{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/916805-alternate-philosophies-for-beast-discussion-thread","id":916805,"title":"Alternate Philosophies for Beast [Discussion Thread]","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Beast: The Primordial"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-06-18T06:52:15-07:00","id":916805,"title":"Alternate Philosophies for Beast [Discussion Thread]","content":"This thread is to discuss alternate philosophies to 'teaching lessons', the lack of which is, in my opinion, possibly Beast's greatest flaw.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/forum/core/<img class=\"bbcode-attachment js-need-data-att-update\" src=\"filedata/fetch?filedataid=423\" data-tempid=\"temp_423_1523360155059_212\" />\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10397-ajf115","name":"ajf115","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10397_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2416"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-18T09:19:15-07:00","id":916866,"title":"","content":"Well, it's also pretty easy to take a social Darwinist view of things when you're a Beast. You're bigger, scarier, and smarter than the humans, so why shouldn't you fill a cave with stolen jewelry?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://eoinsstuff.shoutwiki.com/wiki/Genius:_the_Transgression_(2nd_Edition)\" target=\"_blank\">Genius: the Transgression 2E is a thing that's being worked on.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4488-malonkey1","name":"malonkey1","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4488_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1620"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-18T09:26:47-07:00","id":916872,"title":"","content":"Well, this seems like as good a place as any to really dig into some of the thoughts I have on Beasts and ethics. A lot of this has been on my mind since first reading the original draft, but recent topics have inspired me to try to articulate it for other people. <br /> <br /> Rather than try to talk for the splat as a whole I mostly want to explore this through the lens of one specific character, but let\u2019s start with the one general thing I want to say. A Beast has two parts, Life and Legend, which is to say the Person and the Horror. The Person does <b>NOT</b> have to agree with the Horror. They don\u2019t have to like the Horror, but they have to live with it. One place I think the writers really could have sold the premise better is in presenting more and stronger examples of people in conflict with their Horrors. I get why they didn\u2019t, word count is limited and the Hunger examples need to be quick blurbs that demonstrate how visceral they are. It\u2019s hard to fit resistance into that space. It did, unfortunately, lead to a lot of people coming to the conclusion that Beasts are universally unrepentant abusers. That\u2019s not my reading and I don\u2019t think that was the intent, but I\u2019ll get to that. <br /> <br /> When I say I think Beasts can be good people, I should probably clarify what I mean. Horrors are without exception monsters that want to do harm, that is their nature. A Horror is at best easily placated. The Person, however, has choices. They can choose how they feed, and look for ways to do so as harmlessly as possible. <br /> <br /> Having said that, I want to talk about Drake Thorn. <br /> <br /> Drake hews much more closely to a Homecoming origin than a Devouring. The former always felt more more right to my personal metaphor. This isn\u2019t something that happened to me, a specific occurrence I can point at and say \u201cyeah that\u2019s where it started\u201d It\u2019s something that was always there on some level. Accepting it gave it a name, and allowed me to start connecting a bunch of seemingly unrelated dots. It let me see that there was a community of people with similar struggles I could talk to for support and advice. I\u2019m no longer fighting the monster that is me alone in the dark.<br /> <br /> That\u2019s how it was in Drake\u2019s nightmare. He was the knight in shining armor, and there was the Dragon. Every time they fought he would mortally wound it, only to suffer the same injuries and awake in a cold sweat. For years he refused to see the obvious connection, stuck between choosing life for himself and death for the monster, until one night it finally clicked and he realized it was all the same. The monster he fought was himself. <br /> <br /> Now here\u2019s the thing: Drake has accepted that the Horror is an irrevocable part of him, but he doesn\u2019t like it and he doesn\u2019t agree with it most of the time. The Dragon is a bully, plain and simple. It demands everyone around it bow down in fear. Is it not mighty? Is it not terrifying? Given free reign it would find a village of mortals to terrorize into servitude. But it <i>doesn\u2019t</i> have free reign. Drake is still in the driver\u2019s seat, and the Dragon is going to have to settle for less. He likes helping people move, there\u2019s plenty of opportunity for shock and awe when he singlehandedly lifts furniture too heavy for two normal people to easily move. It\u2019s not a very filling meal for the Dragon at all, but it\u2019s one Drake\u2019s okay with. <br /> <br /> Drake doesn\u2019t have total control either though, and if he balks too much the Dragon goes feeding on its own and attracts Heroes, so it wasn\u2019t long before he had to cut ties with his family and take up a semi nomadic lifestyle. Of course, that makes it harder to find regular meals that won\u2019t strain his ethics. He\u2019s had to do things he\u2019s not happy with to stay fed, but even when picking fights for a quick meal, Drake fights less to injure his opponent and more to demonstrate that they can\u2019t hurt him. It\u2019s in no way a good action but it\u2019s one of the better of his limited options.<br /> <br /> Eventually Drake finds his way the Changeling Freehold of San Francisco, where he takes his current name and pick ups the surname Thorn. It\u2019s an old tradition, started by the Spring Court, that anybody who can\u2019t remember or no longer feels comfortable with the last name they once had can call themselves a Thorn. It\u2019s a big family, and Drake finds his purpose in defending it from the Gentry and their minions. Whatever else it might be, the Dragon really is a powerful monster, and Drake can channel that power. Huntsmen, Hobgoblins slavers, Loyalists, there are any number of enemies looking for cracks in the Bulwark where they can drag one of the Lost back to Arcadia. Drake is strongly opposed to slavery. The Dragon wants to defeat strong enemies and show everyone how powerful it is and how much they should fear it. So when the Huntsmen come calling, Drake\u2019s purpose and his Horror\u2019s align. And that\u2019s a significant point. The Horror is absolutely fighting for selfish, monstrous reasons. But those reasons don\u2019t invalidate Drake\u2019s decision to put his life at risk defending an adopted family, he would do that whether it fed him or not, and he\u2019s never going to shy away from it in favor of an easier meal. The Gentry have hurt his friends, and will keep doing so, and he\u2019s one of relatively few people who have anything resembling the power to prevent them. He will fight a Fae Lord if it comes to that, and despite the extreme danger there is at least a chance (under Equinox Road guidelines anyway) of pulling a \u201cPuny God\u201d moment, and wouldn\u2019t that be a meal. <br /> <br /> Still, he spends a lot of time wondering if deep down he isn\u2019t just as bad of a monster in the end. He wants to become more powerful to better protect his friends, but what happens when he reaches the top and there are no bigger monsters to risk himself against? He doesn\u2019t want to knock the king bullies off their perch only to assume it himself, he wants to empty the perch for good and true, to break the cycle. And his darker moments, he wonders if it wouldn\u2019t be better for everyone if he let himself be killed in the process. Build his friends up enough, and let them finish what he started, because he\u2019s not up to the responsibility of managing the monster inside him and he never really will be. <br /> <br /> There\u2019s two paths for him to take, and that\u2019s the bad one. The good one is something along the lines of the Totem Beast Inheritance that was proposed some threads ago. Drake ascends to the position of something like a guardian spirt for the community and protects it from other supernatural threats. It has been mentioned that Hungers can change, and what he\u2019s attempting is no less than shifting the Dragon to favor a more community friendly flavor of Power. It still demands respect, but that respect is earned and repaid with protection. If need be he can and will demonstrate his power against threats to his charges, but he can also learn to feed off symbolic offerings, given not out of fear but of respect for his power and trust that it will be used in defense of the community. <br /> <br /> No lessons, just a man trying his best to live by the principal that power should be wielded to help people, not hurt them, and that it\u2019s his responsibility to make the best use of his situation. Beast can be a great game to explore what drives people, you just have to look for the disconnect between man and monster.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":8}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5328-helmsderp","name":"HelmsDerp","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"652"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-18T10:25:36-07:00","id":916889,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>malonkey1</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/916805-alternate-philosophies-for-beast-discussion-thread?p=916866#post916866\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, it's also pretty easy to take a social Darwinist view of things when you're a Beast. You're bigger, scarier, and smarter than the humans, so why shouldn't you fill a cave with stolen jewelry?</div> </div> </div> </div>And arguably that's what nature would have intended, what with evolution, survival of the fittest and the strong supplanting the weak, after all. This is one of the reasons I like CoD, you can use traditional 'bad-guy' philosophies with the main characters and explore them. Mind you, that one doesn't do much in the way off justification or granting moral high ground. I could see a character espousing that view as a serious nihilist (in the philosophical sense) or unconventional existentialist.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/forum/core/<img class=\"bbcode-attachment js-need-data-att-update\" src=\"filedata/fetch?filedataid=423\" data-tempid=\"temp_423_1523360155059_212\" />\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10397-ajf115","name":"ajf115","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10397_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2416"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-18T10:55:28-07:00","id":916908,"title":"","content":"At the risk of being obvious - Punishment.<br /> <br /> From the start, this approach allows for a lot of diversity. A Beast could find a way to work fully within constraints of the law, perhaps even as its officer. Further in, they can make plans worthy of Count Monte Cristo, as long as they shy away from committing murder. Such Beasts could easily come out to their human families and even be accepted. Of course, the Horror keeps gnawing at their resolve trying to push them further. At the far end are the more monstrous Beasts - those whose predations end with madness, mutilation or death. Such Beast have a mythological precedent in the Greek Furies. These Monster-Goddesses, while unrepentantly vicious, were highly respected. However, they did play by the rules: they punished acts ancient Greeks denounced as evil, and were one of few sources of justice in a lawless world.<br /> <br /> Even now, depending on where you look, Beasts' brand of justice can be the only one there is. Things are more complicated in lawful areas. Setting aside the state of their psyches, such characters as Dexter Morgan or Frank Castle, even though their victims arguably had it coming, could have worked within the law, but didn't. Moral high ground is harder to reach now than it was in the Iron Age. Their cases also show the risks of trying to hold to one's Life, or risks of leaving it behind. <br /> <br /> While playing such characters can be deeply cathartic, care must be taken to show the consequences of such feedings. Even punishing real crimes has consequences that the Beast would rather not have to face. This goes double for crimes that society turns a blind eye to - well meaning Beasts could create martyrs for the wrong causes or even provoke retaliation against victims. Even that assumes that Beasts choose objectively evil acts to punish. Even ordinary human beings have drastically different opinions about what acts are criminal. Becoming a Beast does not necessarily help grow past that.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>~<span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\">\u266b</span></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7593-teatime","name":"Teatime","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7593_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"750"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-18T11:18:59-07:00","id":916916,"title":"","content":"The Social Darwinist attitude is the default of Horrors and Beasts, and it gets pretty boring. Rather like a vampire who's just vampiring it up, there comes a time after the hundredth or so time of feeding that a Beast is probably going to go &quot;Fuck, is this it?&quot;. The Teaching Culture(and whatever else this thread comes up with) isn't just a moral fix, it's a way for Beasts to feel like what they're doing matters rather than just rutting around.<br /> <br /> On Punishment, I gotta say this isn't new-most peoples ideas for lessons and proper eating basically begins and ends with &quot;Do it to those who deserve it.&quot;<br /> <br /> It's been brought up how Beasts have Lives and Legends. Worth noting is that the teaching culture provides an avenue that allows for a balancing of those two. Alternatives don't have to strive for balance, but accordance to one, the other, or both should be kept in mind.<br /> <br /> The name of the game is &quot;How do I take meaningless feeding and turn it into a meaningful contribution to X?&quot;, X being self, mortal society, supernatural society, etc., etc.<br /> <br /> Let's see what y'all can come up with. This should be interesting.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-18T12:17:54-07:00","id":916939,"title":"","content":"Among other major problems with the &quot;teaching lessons&quot; theme, I can never take it seriously because it always ends up making me think of Arrested Development. <br /> <br /> What Family would J. Walter Weatherman be","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5358-geckopirateship","name":"Geckopirateship","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5358_2.gif","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1383"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-18T12:41:00-07:00","id":916946,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/916805-alternate-philosophies-for-beast-discussion-thread?p=916916#post916916\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">On Punishment, I gotta say this isn't new-most peoples ideas for lessons and proper eating basically begins and ends with &quot;Do it to those who deserve it.&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>You know, I think you're right. It's fair to say that Punishment is a subset of Lesson-teaching. However, that umbrella term is broad enough to cover very different attitudes - some more alien than the others. Whether we come up with a truly alternate philosophy or with a distinctive approach to Lesson-teaching, we accomplish the same goal: We give players an idea on what can they do, exactly. I think this might be the reason why some people are frustrated with the present approach. It doesn't explain how Lesson-teaching works in practice and how many forms it can take. We have a very general theory and many specific examples, but little on how Beasts connect the two and what motivates them to choose one approach over the other.<br /> <br /> For example, targeting the deserving is readily understandable and most cases fit under the Lesson-teaching umbrella. However, if the target doesn't survive and the act is done in secret, there is nobody to have learned anything. On the other hand, a Beast may deliberately target innocents, trying to create martyrs. Considering how tragedy can bring people together and prompt changes for the better, a Beast may consider it a fair trade. The period after Kennedy's assassination was far more progressive than the one before it. This definitely fits under Lesson-teaching, while in some respects being the very opposite of Punishment.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>~<span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\">\u266b</span></span>","edited":{"userid":7593,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7593-teatime","user":"Teatime","date":". 06-18-2016, 03:45 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7593-teatime","name":"Teatime","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7593_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"750"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-18T12:47:20-07:00","id":916948,"title":"","content":"During the games games I played two distinct &quot;philosophies&quot; emerged amongst the players. The first one is to do as little harm as possible while feeding. Xhemal - an anakim tyrant - feeds by completely dominating his opponents, by defeating them in a way that shows that they never had a chance to begin with and could just as well forfeit. He is playing soccer and feeds every time he successfully seizes the ball (Cyclopean strength helps). Xhemal is aware that as he ages he will either lose his physical strength (preventing him from feeding this way) or keep it thanks to his supernatural origin (thus alerting anyone that he is supernatural) and won't be able to feed this He is studying law and for now feeds on the professors and students by solving the most difficult questions in the most detailed way possible that should be impossible for someone in the first semester (Thank you Mimir's Wisdom). Once he finishes his studies he'll become a lawyer (not a judge as a judge doesn't have to fight an opponent) and keep on feeding this way.<br /> <br /> Of course not everyone can feed in such a &quot;vegetarian&quot; way, and this was especially true for two other characters: David - an Eshmaki predator - feeds on stalking people from the shadows and hunting them down until they believe they're going to die. He doesn't have to kill them but all of them are seriously injured. Connor - an Ugallu ravager - feeds by destroying the very idea that anywhere would be safe. His homecoming happened in Afghanistan where he served as a sniper - and everyone was impressed by his ability to kill people who thought themselves perfectly safe behind cover. As a civilian this ability is much less appreciated. Connor does not have to kill people - a bullet passing over the table of a dining family and impacting in the wall behind them destroys the safety good enough - but he always has to traumatize his victims. Xhemal did try to get them to feed in a paint ball arena but both found the meals there bland and unsatisfying.<br /> <br /> Apart from the obvious conflicts within the brood this also gave rise to a second philosophy: Rather than trying to minimize the harm done during feeding they accepted it but they tried to do as much good with the unique abilities they had as beasts so the society as a whole would still be better off. Kind of like one accepts the (undeniably bad) side effects of medications because one is still better off than without. David became a fire-fighter and saves people from burning houses without any concern for his own safety - after all his relentless hunter atavism will take care of any injuries he sustains in no time. He still needs to hunt down and injure people to feed his horror but figures that the people he saves from dying in a fire make up for that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10574-matthiasthalmann","name":"MatthiasThalmann","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"35"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-18T13:04:43-07:00","id":916953,"title":"","content":"My bigger point is that a Punishment culture isn't exactly a new idea. Batman was the first reconciliation people had with Beast and remains one of people's favorite icons for the games. There's nothing wrong with that, and it's easy to make sense of it in the mythos, metaphysics and sociology of Beast, but it's not new.<br /> <br /> EDIT: Also, I find a Punishment culture more terrifying than a Teaching culture.<br /> <br /> Let's not get hung up on this.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-18T13:22:36-07:00","id":916961,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar99_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"99\" data-vbnamecard=\"99\">ArcaneArts</a>: Didn't you post a quote in other threads with a phrase along the lines of &quot;people don't hate monsters, they love us, they've always told stories about us&quot;? Could you please repost it? I find it relevant to this discussion.<br /> <br /> I think one of the most productive alternate philosophies is one of improvement in the face of adversity, as that quote neatly described. This is hinted at in the section for Tyrants, but I believe it really deserves more exploration. There's the Totem Inheritance that HelmsDerp mentioned (which can be found <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/852054-totem-beasts\"><b>here</b></a>), but the Beast doesn't necessarily have to play the role of protector.<br /> <br /> Not all environments provide the same opportunities for this, however. It's possible that a Beast CEO can motivate rival companies to get their act together, but not all of them will react with the same tactics. Some will genuinely improve their teamwork, become more perceptive and so on, while others will crumble under the weight of the competition, pettiness and in-fighting.<br /> <br /> I would also be quite interested in seeing how Hungers other than Power could be used for this.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Bye.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4443-gibberingeloquence","name":"GibberingEloquence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4443_5.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2470"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-18T13:26:50-07:00","id":916964,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>GibberingEloquence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/916805-alternate-philosophies-for-beast-discussion-thread?p=916961#post916961\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar99_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"99\" data-vbnamecard=\"99\">ArcaneArts</a>: Didn't you post a quote in other threads with a phrase along the lines of &quot;people don't hate monsters, they love us, they've always told stories about us&quot;? Could you please repost it? I find it relevant to this discussion.<br /> <br /> I think one of the most productive alternate philosophies is one of improvement in the face of adversity, as that quote neatly described. This is hinted at in the section for Tyrants, but I believe it really deserves more exploration. There's the Totem Inheritance that HelmsDerp mentioned (which can be found <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/852054-totem-beasts\"><b>here</b></a>), but the Beast doesn't necessarily have to play the role of protector.<br /> <br /> Not all environments provide the same opportunities for this, however. It's possible that a Beast CEO can motivate rival companies to get their act together, but not all of them will react with the same tactics. Some will genuinely improve their teamwork, become more perceptive and so on, while others will crumble under the weight of the competition, pettiness and in-fighting.<br /> <br /> I would also be quite interested in seeing how Hungers other than Power could be used for this.</div> </div> </div> </div>Here ya go.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dresden Files, Ghost Story, Page 130</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Her dimples deepened. 'Why are you so upset, young shade? I really don't see anything wrong with being a monster.&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;I do,&quot; I said. &quot;The monster part?&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;Oh,&quot; the girl said, shaking her head. &quot;Don't be so simple. People adore monsters. They fill their songs and stories with them. They define themselves in relation to them. Do you know what a monster is, young shade? Power. Power and choice. Monsters make choices. Monsters shape the world. Monsters force us to become stronger, smarter, better. They sift the weak from the strong and provide a forge for the steeling of souls. Even as we curse monsters, we admire them. Seek to become them, in some ways.&quot; Her eyes became distant. &quot;There are far, far worse things to be than a monster.&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>Mind you, this quote has a lot of ties to the teaching culture, which this thread is trying to explore away from.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-18T13:46:46-07:00","id":916972,"title":"","content":"Ok, so some ideas for other approaches:<br /> <br /> <b>The Dream Masters</b> -<br /> <br /> Most likely to develop in Beasts that are naturally fairly intellectually inclined, or those that spend a lot of time forming Kinship bonds with others of that bend (esp. mages and changelings), this approach goes beyond &quot;teaching lessons.&quot; This philosophy assumes that as Horrors are born of the Primordial Dream, they are driven by Hungers because of the darkness of the world. Dreams are manifestations of the subconscious desires of humanity. They think the Lesson Teachers are simply shallowly responding to a desire from humanity for the world to &quot;make sense&quot; where evil is done unto evil to put humanity back on a brighter path. The Dream Masters want to be more proactive, and want to work towards reshaping the Primordial Dream into something &quot;better&quot; rather than accepting it as fact; feeling that this will eventually change Hungers into less negative expressions of humanity's desires.<br /> <br /> In practice this is rarely a simple matter. The desire to focus on dreams - and Nightmare powers - requires a well fed Beast that means indulging the Hungers as-is. Dream Masters do their best to make their feeding more than just a lesson that humans have a reason to fear the darkness of the world, but to be &quot;tough love&quot; that is more guided towards a healthy respect for the state of the world that hopefully leads to humans wanting a brighter world, and thus the inertia of the Primordial Dream will shift to &quot;better&quot; Hungers.<br /> <br /> <b>The Hyenas</b><br /> <br /> A more organized group, most Beats come to this approach by recruitment; though many were on their way there without the aid. First and foremost, the Hyenas are practical. Worrying about &quot;why&quot; and mortality is always second to survival, and keeping their Hungers in check. In the wild, hyenas are social animals that have to deal with an apex predator that's also a social animal as competition. No hyena can stand up to a lion, but a single lion can't stand up to a full clan, and even a pride gets nervous against such numbers. The Hyenas see Beasts as existing in the same state. The shadows are full of monsters, and the Apex dominates the local Dream that everyone else has to deal with. Beasts - and their Kin - are all children of the Dark Mother, should be creating families large enough to remind any Apex that they are never invincible. <br /> <br /> Feeding beyond necessity is a matter of tactics; a form of political and social control over the local supernatural landscape with humans as the battleground. &quot;Lessons&quot; aren't the point compared to engineering social norms and/or changes. Every Apex has a pyramid beneath them, and the Hyenas see feeding as a tool to tear that pyramid out from under them if they don't treat the status of Apex as the Hyenas feel it should be, or work to support what they see as a &quot;proper&quot; Apex. If a Hyena manages to take on this mantle... things often get messy since the group is so self-associated with needing to be the masses that keep the powerful in check for their own good.<br /> <br /> <b>The Meal</b><br /> <br /> To many Beasts, Meals are worse than those that simply aim to be amoral monsters in the shadows. They're fanatics with a dangerous faith, which is often true, though some are rather scary in how aware of what they're doing they really are. The core of this faith is simple: The Dark Mother is ravenous, and her children are not keeping her well fed. Eventually her hunger will drive her children and humanity insane, so the only option is to full sate her so she truly slumbers as a well fed Horror does. They see a simple pattern: their Horror drives them to Hunger, and unless they put their Horror to sleep with a good enough meal, Heroes are inevitable. They don't see Heroes as true enemies - as humans rising up to fight off predators - but as lesser children born to drive Beasts to greater power, so they can feed their Hungers more, which feeds the Dark Mother more.<br /> <br /> The solution is simple. Just as fully sating a Hunger requires refined and thoughtful feeding, the Dark Mother requires more than the trickles of Satiety. She needs bigger meals. She needs monsters to step out of the shadows to terrify whole cities. She needs humanity to rip itself apart from the terror of the truth. This isn't a lesson, but an offering of the greatest levels. The Meal will save humanity by ending the cycle of Beasts and Heroes after an age of horrors that leaves the Dark Mother so full she'll never wake again.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-18T17:27:05-07:00","id":917054,"title":"","content":"Another idea is that the Begotten are born to unify the creatures of darkness, bringing their Kin under one banner, whether to protect themselves or to claim the world as their own,","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3514-zealouschristian24","name":"ZealousChristian24","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1796"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-19T02:42:39-07:00","id":917263,"title":"","content":"One idea I had for an alternate goal for Beasts was that they essentially acted as the Monsters in Black.<br /> <br /> What happens when humans interact with the supernatural? They run the risk of losing their minds, losing their souls, losing their lives.<br /> <br /> Beasts are about fear, and what is the point of fear? Telling you not to do something- don't play in traffic, don't touch the hot stove, don't pet the snarling dog.<br /> <br /> So it's a symbiotic relationship- Monsters get less pitchforks and torch mobs, less humans go insane/soulless/eaten, and humans live to fear another day so Beasts can eat. Win-win, unless you're one of those humans who are going to go insane/get eaten/have their souls consumed, but them's the breaks. <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/391-stsword","name":"StSword","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar391_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1635"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-19T16:43:24-07:00","id":917624,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ZealousChristian24</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/916805-alternate-philosophies-for-beast-discussion-thread?p=917054#post917054\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Another idea is that the Begotten are born to unify the creatures of darkness, bringing their Kin under one banner, whether to protect themselves or to claim the world as their own,</div> </div> </div> </div>This would have been great. As it stands, there's a reason for Beasts to go find other player splats, but not much reason for other monsters to bother with Beasts or treat them as anything other than a dangerous nosy mystery. If Beast had something going on that the other player splats would be concerned about, to flesh out a reason for a bunch of random folks from different gamelines to pull together, it could have been THE crossover Chronicles of Darkness gameline.<br /> <br /> As for Monsters in Black... well, the tricky part there is that Beasts NEED people to &quot;misbehave&quot; if they're going to take that route. The last lesson they'll ever teach is &quot;here's how to cooperate with each other to find and kill me&quot;.<br /> <br /> The most compelling idea I've been able to find for Beast is that it's about alienation. Prometheans are alienated but they have a way back &quot;in&quot;; Beasts don't. Beasts aren't just the monster out beyond the campfire; they're also the monster that gets chased down and triumphantly killed. They are Not On Humanity's Side. Their Horror knows that and so do the humans (represented by the heroes), it's the Beast themself that needs to grapple with that; whether that means finding a new family they can live with, throwing themself into predation, or trying to mediate with the enemy and probably screw it up.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11959-niknokk","name":"Niknokk","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"68"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-19T17:19:54-07:00","id":917647,"title":"","content":"Mother's Kiss and Family Ties both offer reasonably potent benefits for other supernats. Beasts can force open passages to other planes of reality, useful for escape as well as invasion. Beyond that, Beasts are just powerful monsters with a versatile tool kit of powers available to them. It's a dangerous world and everybody has enemies, that alone is reason enough to consider Beasts as ally potential. Don't forget companionship either. Supernatural types are isolated from the vast bulk of humanity by their otherness, and their own kinds tend to be few in number. This is more true of some splats than others, but finding somebody who understands you, the part of you that you can't show to most of the world, can be a pretty powerful motivator.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5328-helmsderp","name":"HelmsDerp","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"652"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-19T17:25:03-07:00","id":917648,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Niknokk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/916805-alternate-philosophies-for-beast-discussion-thread?p=917624#post917624\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This would have been great. As it stands, there's a reason for Beasts to go find other player splats, but not much reason for other monsters to bother with Beasts or treat them as anything other than a dangerous nosy mystery.</div> </div> </div> </div>A Gorged Beast is a tractable amplifier on your supernatural impact that gets stronger from you forcing them to do things that aren't good for them and the entire deal with Family Ties is that any given Beast is a unifying thematic nexus for their kin.<br /> <br /> As assets for an army of darkness go you can do a-lot-a-lot worse than claiming the banner of a Begotten herald.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-19T17:54:17-07:00","id":917664,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/916805-alternate-philosophies-for-beast-discussion-thread?p=917648#post917648\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A Gorged Beast is a tractable amplifier on your supernatural impact that gets stronger from you forcing them to do things that aren't good for them</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't follow who &quot;you&quot; and &quot;them&quot; are. Can you clarify?<br /> <br /> There are mechanical benefits to hanging out with a Beast, sure. Narratively, to an established group, they're an unknown risk. Also, &quot;we're family, and I'm the elder brother&quot; has got to run thin pretty fast. In a Beast-centric game that works out, you can hang out with outcasts and folks who got rejected by their other societies. Vampires on the outs with the Prince, Ghost Wolves, Apostate mages, changelings unwelcome at Freeholds, it works.<br /> Every other gameline is perfectly aware that they're living in A World of Darkness, they don't have all the answers, the questions can kill them, and there is always something out there willing to and capable of eating them. Beast is the only gameline with that &quot;we're all family!&quot; thing going on, and I don't get the insistence that the other organized groups would naturally play along with that.<br /> ison.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11959-niknokk","name":"Niknokk","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"68"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-19T18:13:34-07:00","id":917682,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Niknokk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/916805-alternate-philosophies-for-beast-discussion-thread?p=917664#post917664\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't follow who &quot;you&quot; and &quot;them&quot; are. Can you clarify?</div> </div> </div> </div>&quot;You&quot; is a supernatural with any sort of mental influence power. &quot;Them&quot; is the Gorged Beast.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Also, &quot;we're family, and I'm the elder brother&quot; has got to run thin pretty fast. </div> </div> </div>Neither a universal nor especially common behavior and the Apex of a region tends to be respected by most local Begotten who aren't aiming for the spot themselves, which is a major example of exactly what I'm getting at with the whole &quot;getting a Beast under your sway is a handy route to power and influence&quot; thing.<br /> <br /> This is a Philosophies thread, not a Perfect Way To Run The Game thread. Attempting to dissect a statement made in support for a proposed in-universe philosophy on the basis of your problems with the game itself is outside the scope of the topic.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Every other gameline is perfectly aware that they're living in A World of Darkness, they don't have all the answers, the questions can kill them, and there is always something out there willing to and capable of eating them. Beast is the only gameline with that &quot;we're all family!&quot; thing going on, and I don't get the insistence that the other organized groups would naturally play along with that. </div> </div> </div>You're going to have to explain where anyone is insisting anything, because the Children's claims of family are statements of relation and commonality more than a claim that they have all the answers.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":111,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","user":"Satchel","date":". 06-19-2016, 09:16 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-19T18:34:36-07:00","id":917698,"title":"","content":"Thanks for clarifying, I didn't expect that twist. I do think that putting together a family of outcasts would work pretty well as a philosophy, actually.<br /> <br /> Had another idea, specifically for collectors: Help Me Help You (help me): Collectors have a problem with letting things go but can be salved with something more valuable than what they lost. They could maybe also get around that issue via a &quot;loan&quot; with a steep enough interest rate. The Templars were banker-knights, imagine if they had a dragon as an investor. (Or you could go with the obvious loan-shark image). Anyway, bottom line; client gets a service, collector gets a meal. Would work about equally well for an actual loan-shark or a magic item/old secrets hoard where the entry fee was more of the same.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11959-niknokk","name":"Niknokk","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"68"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-19T21:53:10-07:00","id":917791,"title":"","content":"I think that when it comes to Begotten Philosophy that to some degree it all should come down to defining what aspects of a nightmare the Children should exhibit. <br /> <br /> The reason why I love the inclusion of lessons in the final version of Beast is because it's a steppingstone to explore the nature of nightmares and how they have supernatural depth and substance in the world of Chronicles of Darkness. It's the reason why we love horror stories, and what we take away from such tales even when there might not be any intended morals. At it's core, the subversive themes of Beast gives us as players a lens to dissect and examine what elements we love most about good horror and most importantly why we like them.<br /> <br /> For example, one familiar option and three less so:<br /> <br /> Visionaries, Nightmares as Lessons: Humanity sometimes forgets. They forget the importance of family, friends, health, dreams, and the value of their own short lives. It's not always their fault, life has a way of getting away from oneself sometimes. They blink and they're old, and all that's left is regret for the roads not taken. You'll make sure that doesn't happen to them. Tonight they'll remember what it's like to run like the first time they knew fear. Tonight they'll struggle and cry like they haven't since adulthood stripped away their imagination of the unknown. Tonight they will fall and bleed and pray to powers they had given up believing in when the world became a place without meaning. But, they will wake up. They will survive. They will see the snow covered grave and cry out &quot;Is this a vision of what will be? Or what may be?!?&quot; And you will feed. They will not take their life for granted for a very long time. <br /> <br /> Emissaries, Nightmares as Mirrors: They see something different. Every single one of them. The human sees just another human, ordinary if not perculiar in their individual ways. Look to close into their eyes or whisper just the right word, and they'll see the monster beneath. The monster that any human could become under the right circumstance. The supernatural creatures see themselves too, except for the particularly canny. It's never a perfect reflection though. The vampire sees a dead thing like themselves, always thirsty and able to blend in among the prey. But the thing inside isn't the slightest bit human, maybe never was. The witch sees the arcane aura similar to their own, and recognizes the desire for knowledge and understanding. But the aura unnerves them in it's hunger in a way they don't yet understand, like a truth that is until it isn't. What about themselves? What does the Beast see when it looks deep within itself? Well, it sees the broken reflections of the waking world. A dark mirror of a dark world. Beautiful in it's cruelty, terrifying in it's scope. Horror is in the eye of the beholder, and the reflection is endlessly hungry for something to give it form and substance. <br /> <br /> Pragmatists, Nightmares as Truths: There are a few simple truths in this world. They aren't pretty. They aren't kind. But they are true, and whether you like it or not one of those truths are &quot;Everyone's got to eat.&quot; That doesn't necessarily make you cruel. Nore does it mean you have to be gluttonous. It also doesn't mean you have to use proper table manners either. Nightmares and dreams are one and the same, the only difference is who's getting devoured and who's the one chowing down. Why not use your powers to make life comfortable? Why not use it to seize power? Under any other circumstance the prey would use it to do the same to you...well ok, that's not necessarily true. Everyone does have to eat though. Nothing's going to change that. Why not embrace that truth rather than suffering in humane martyrdom. That's a lifestyle choice. Hunger isn't.<br /> <br /> Celebrants, Nightmares as Legacy: Here's the thing about dreams and nightmares, they're fleeting. Try remembering the last dream you had. Last nightmare? No? That's not a suprise. Dreams are fleeting because life is fleeting. We're born, live our lives for as long as we can, and then die. It doesn't have to be that way though. A part of one's self can survive beyond the veil of death. Shape the lives of countless humans and more. The dream need not die with the dreamer, nor the nightmare. The Legend holds the seeds of immortality. The Primordial Dream is shaped by the ancient shadows of monsters and fiends long gone, but who's actions and deeds still haunt the dreams of mortal minds. The hunger pushes one towards apotheosis, to a state of divinity impossible to obtain through mortal means. Follow the footsteps of those who devoured before you. Seek to become the Incarnant Beast. Become Myth, else all you are pass into eternity with the rising sun.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3436,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3436-dusksage","user":"Dusksage","date":".  Clarification 06-20-2016, 01:20 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3436-dusksage","name":"Dusksage","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3436_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"869"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-20T03:43:09-07:00","id":917916,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Niknokk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/916805-alternate-philosophies-for-beast-discussion-thread?p=917624#post917624\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As for Monsters in Black... well, the tricky part there is that Beasts NEED people to &quot;misbehave&quot; if they're going to take that route. The last lesson they'll ever teach is &quot;here's how to cooperate with each other to find and kill me.&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div> What in the world are you talking about? People who get killed by the supernatural are no longer viable sources of food. That's like arguing that shephards need their sheep to get eaten by wild animals. <br /> <br /> As for your second point, yes, because fearmongering is so ineffective, that's why people don't do it anymore after 200 thousand years of human history, no wait, people still do fearmongering because it works.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/391-stsword","name":"StSword","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar391_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1635"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-20T09:43:19-07:00","id":918084,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Niknokk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/916805-alternate-philosophies-for-beast-discussion-thread?p=917664#post917664\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Beast is the only gameline with that &quot;we're all family!&quot; thing going on, and I don't get the insistence that the other organized groups would naturally play along with that.</div> </div> </div> </div>Just on the face of it, there is no reason why they necessarily should. A Beast who takes family with supernaturals as their philosophical foundation - at least a sensible Beast who does so - won't be blind to that, and consider it their responsibility to <i>provide</i> reasons for other supernaturals to accept them and one another. Maybe they'll fail, but it's certainly a viable thing to try.<br /> My own approach to a &quot;we are family&quot; philosophy is generally to act as a mediator between factions.<br /> <br /> Others...<br /> <br /> One of the things that struck me about the first draft (a bit less about the second, but not entirely gone) was that despite its outre protagonists, Beast was good for a game where supernaturals continued to live very mortal lives, and engage with the world in very mortal ways - partly because feeding, at least in small meals, can often be carried out via behaviours that wouldn't considered monstrous in humans (and yet since they're of a continuum with more horrific feeding, good for looking at the strands of power and abuse that are accepted parts of human life).<br /> Thus, I like the idea of an Isolate philosophy, who consider their human life (and Life) to remain their &quot;real&quot; life, and continue living it as much as possible. They might still dig into the supernatural world around them, but in the ways mortals might, and perhaps more focussed on phenomena than beings. Not rejecting their supernature, just not using their supernature as a reason to reject their former lives.<br /> <br /> I also considered a Ravener faction - who might be disparagingly called Gluttons - who respond to the &quot;is this all there is&quot; question with &quot;maybe, maybe not, but either way, it's good and true and right.&quot; Feeding their Hungers is a right thing, and they do so without guilt, shame, or remorse. But not necessarily without conscience or restraint - feeding might be a right thing, but it doesn't have to be the only right thing. I guess these would be the ones who take to heart that Dresden Files quote above, or a similar one from the earlier Nightbreed - but part of that power and choice is that they're not even forced to abandon love or morals - unless they want to.<br /> And they focus on their Legends the way the Isolates focus on their Lives. Some of them would pursue Incarnation as their ultimate goal.<br /> <br /> I hadn't really considered something like Heavy Arms's Meals, but I like the idea. Beasts are Mouths, and they exist to keep the Dark Mother fed. As well as big meals, they might also try to vary their feedings as much as possible - metaphorically make sure all her vitamins and cravings are satisfied.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/321-sunlessnick","name":"SunlessNick","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1214"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-27T20:02:48-07:00","id":922388,"title":"","content":"My thinking was that rather than &quot;teaching lessons,&quot; both Beasts and Heroes are basically actors in reenactments of ancient, memetic stories. The struggle for Beasts and Heroes would be trying to balance their human sensibilities, and acting out their roles in the larger story. Beasts and Heroes aren't supposed to teach lessons on their own- rather, they tell stories together, to those who watch their interactions. Of course, some Beasts and Heroes don't understand that. <br /> <br /> I hit on this idea as part of my musings on how to introduce a morality stat into Beast, which I feel is kind of lacking.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1122743-haberdasher-s-requiem-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#B22222\">Haberdasher's Requiem Conversions and Homebrew</span></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6498-haberdasher","name":"Haberdasher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6498_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"818"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-27T20:34:54-07:00","id":922413,"title":"","content":"Running off of a notion I've been chewing over for the last few days:<br /> <br /> The <b>Night Parade</b> take something of a pragmatic view to supernatural life. Not for nothing, they reason, are the most legendary monsters among their kin also the most well-traveled. The territoriality of the Horror is a trap; the Lair is all a Beast should need as far as a place to call their own, and the Apex is a role too pinned down by geography to be worth claiming.<br /> <br /> The family is their territory, they say, unbound by state or sea or shadow, and so the Harbingers aim to be the most gregarious of the Begotten, forming wandering broods and hunting across the myriad lands and worlds of the Chronicles of Darkness in the pursuit of new sympathies with which to grow the social network through which they feed and meet new people as they live the lives of Strangers in strange lands.<br /> <br /> Most of all, say the eldest among them, it is important that the night move on with the dawn. The Children are the scions of a dynasty on which the sun never need rise; it's always midnight somewhere, after all.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":111,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","user":"Satchel","date":". 06-28-2016, 12:17 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-28T07:35:36-07:00","id":922608,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by 